  "read": {
    "headline": "The week every Treasury from two years out went through 5%.",
    "standfirst": "Weekend edition. Thursday closed flat — the S&P 500 lost 0.02% to 7,704.13, the Nasdaq was unchanged at 26,939.37, the Dow fell 0.31% for a third straight day — but the week belonged to the bond market. The ten-year went from 4.95% on Monday to 5.18% on Thursday, its highest since 2007; the thirty-year to 5.47%, its highest since 2004; and three coupon auctions cleared at yields their maturities had not paid in two decades. Brent round-tripped from $100 to $99 and back to $106 on Iran; the dollar hit an eight-week high; utilities lost 4.23% on the week and technology gained 2.70%. The board is as of Thursday's close and Friday's early trade, and holds until Monday.",
    "points": [
      {
        "title": "The week the whole Treasury curve went through five.",
        "text": "The two-year is 4.87%, the five-year 5.03%, the ten-year 5.18% and the thirty-year 5.47% — every point from two years out now yields five or more, for the first time since 2007. Over the month the two-year is up 16.23% and the ten-year 11.16%. The long Treasury ETF lost 2.25% on the week and is down 10.74% over twelve months; investment-grade credit is off 7.12%. Three auctions cleared — two-years at 4.787%, five-years at 5.033%, seven-years at 5.085% — each at a yield the maturity had not paid in at least two decades, and each with a little less interest than the one before."
      },
      {
        "title": "Utilities lost 4.23% on the week. That is the rates trade in one row.",
        "text": "The three rate-sensitive sectors did the falling: utilities −4.23% over five days, financials −2.38%, real estate −2.07%. Technology rose 2.70% and communication services 2.87%, so the S&P 500 ETF finished the week up 0.72% while most of its constituents fell. Utilities are now down 14.81% over three months and 7.80% on the year, the worst sector on both windows; financials at −0.44% year to date are the only other one under water. Energy, up 40.01%, and technology, up 35.24%, are the year."
      },
      {
        "title": "Brent finished the week higher. WTI finished it 7.79% lower. Both are true.",
        "text": "Brent is $105.01, up 1.10% over five days, after a round trip from $100.34 on Monday to $99.25 on Tuesday and $106.41 on Thursday. WTI shows −7.79% because its front month rolled from October to November mid-week, and the November contract sits about $5 under where October settled — the roll is the backwardation, the premium for barrels now. Over the month Brent is up 19.55% and over the year 71.81%. Natural gas, up 13.12% on the week, is the one energy contract that never priced any de-escalation at all."
      },
      {
        "title": "Emerging markets gave a week back in two days.",
        "text": "South Africa fell 3.78% over five days, Indonesia 3.37%, Argentina 3.09% and Turkey 1.85%; the EM ETF is up 0.33% on the week only because Korea and Taiwan carried it. Korea itself is down 1.68% on the day and 7.48% over three months while up 128.48% over twelve — the most volatile row on the board in both directions. The dollar did it: an eight-week high on Wednesday, the euro down 2.39% and sterling 2.77% over the month, the peso 4.44% weaker. A Treasury sell-off is a dollar bid, and a dollar bid is an EM sale."
      },
      {
        "title": "Gold lost 2.80% in a week when stocks and bonds both fell.",
        "text": "Gold is $4,301, down 2.80% over five days and 7.57% over a month; silver is off 4.56% and 11.97% year to date; platinum −17.96% on the year. A week in which Treasuries had their worst day since April 2025 and the S&P 500 barely moved was exactly the week a hedge should have earned its keep, and the metals did not. Copper, flat on the day and up 42.27% over twelve months, remains the metal that has worked — as an industrial input, not a store of value."
      },
      {
        "title": "Litecoin is up 40% in a month. Nothing else on the board is close.",
        "text": "Litecoin gained 20.78% over five days and 40.42% over a month, the largest moves in either window anywhere on the board; Chainlink is the only major coin positive on the year, at 6.38%. Bitcoin at $84,271 is down 0.13% on the day and up 3.83% on the week, and was the one large asset that did not sell off on Wednesday. Every major coin bar Chainlink is still negative year to date, and Cardano is down 67.58% over twelve months. The five-day column is a squeeze; the one-year column is the trend."
      }
    ]
  },
